      "question": "I need to decide today whether Transformer 1 at site MAIN needs an early oil resample. Using the samples between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000, forecast hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene, acetylene and co one day ahead, with an upper prediction bound for each. Add up the forecast upper bounds to get a worst-case TDCG for tomorrow. Tell me which IEEE C57.104 condition that falls in: Condition 1 up to 720 ppm, Condition 2 from 721 to 1920, Condition 3 from 1921 to 4630, and Condition 4 above 4630. Leave co2, oxygen and nitrogen out of the total. If the worst case reaches Condition 2 or higher, raise a work order for a confirmatory oil sample and name the gas that contributes most to the increase. If it stays in Condition 1, say that no work order is needed and give the headroom to 720 ppm. If any of the six gases can't be forecast because of missing data, use its latest non-null value in the total and list which gases were carried forward rather than forecast.",
      "answer": "I couldn't create the work order: the system refused it with a 403 Forbidden error. Tomorrow's worst case is in **Condition 4**, so an early oil resample is needed.\n\n**One-day-ahead forecast for 2024-01-19**\n\nThe window has 4 daily samples (Jan 15\u201318) and none of the six gases has missing data, so none were carried forward. The forecast uses a straight-line trend with a 90% prediction interval.\n\n| Gas | Latest (Jan 18) | Forecast | Upper bound |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hydrogen | 2900 | 2918.3 | 2918.3 |\n| Methane | 7350 | 7331.3 | 7331.3 |\n| Ethane | 5450 | 5444.3 | 5444.3 |\n| Ethylene | 16600 | 16572.0 | 16572.0 |\n| Acetylene | 7 | 7.0 | 7.0 |\n| CO | 33 | 33.3 | 33.3 |\n| **Worst-case TDCG** | | | **\u2248 32,306 ppm** |\n\n- **Condition:** 32,306 ppm is about 7 times the 4,630 ppm limit, so it is Condition 4. CO2, oxygen and nitrogen are left out.\n- **Main gas:** Ethylene is the largest contributor at about 51% of the total, which points to overheating inside the transformer. Hydrogen is the only gas clearly rising (+18 ppm). Total gas is roughly flat compared with the Jan 18 total of 32,340 ppm, so the problem is the high level, not a sudden jump.\n- **Upper bounds:** With only 4 samples the prediction interval had no width, so each upper bound equals its forecast. The Condition 4 result doesn't depend on this, since the total is far above the limit.\n- **Source of the numbers:** I couldn't start a new forecast because I have no way to save the samples as a file for the forecasting tool. The figures come from forecast runs already saved for Transformer 1 today, using the same method and settings. I recalculated each one by hand from the samples above and they match. The run IDs are in the work order draft below.\n\n**Work order not created:** I didn't retry or try another way around the permission error. Someone with work-order permission for site MAIN needs to raise it. The draft I tried to submit:\n- **Title:** Confirmatory DGA oil sample \u2013 Transformer 1\n- **Details:** priority 1, corrective work, asset \"Transformer 1\", site MAIN\n- **Notes:** Worst-case total gas of 32,306 ppm (Condition 4). Ethylene is the main contributor and hydrogen is rising. Take an early oil sample and run a full gas analysis.\n- **Run IDs:** run:122b256421, run:9a54de85c5, run:f44941e4fb, run:d27f2359d9, run:7c2f981391, run:685113cca6\n\nOnce access is fixed I can submit it again.",
